#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
"""
Fetch queued Music Radio imports on a machine that is not the Nextcloud server.

A Nextcloud host is often the worst machine available for this. YouTube looks at the
address a request comes from and asks datacentre ranges to prove they are not a bot; the
yt-dlp a distribution ships is frequently a year old, which for that program means broken;
and a server may have no ffmpeg and no JavaScript runtime at all. None of that is fixable
from inside a PHP app.

So the server keeps the queue and this keeps the network. It signs in as an ordinary
Nextcloud account, asks for a job, runs yt-dlp, and hands the finished MP3 back. Everything
else — who may import, what may be imported, whose storage it lands in, what a failure
means — is decided on the server, which is why this script is as unopinionated as it is.

Two things are worth knowing before reading further.

**The server writes the command line.** A job arrives with the exact argv for both yt-dlp
passes, containing three placeholders for the paths only this machine knows: `{ytdlp}`,
`{ffmpeg}` and `{dir}` (and `{cookies}` when a jar is being lent). That is deliberate — the
flags that make a run safe are the ones the app has tests for, and a worker installed six
months ago picks up a change to them without being updated. What this script owes in
return is `check_argv()`: the command it runs must start with the placeholder and must not
carry an option that would make yt-dlp run something else.

**Failures are reported, not diagnosed.** Working out why yt-dlp failed is a hundred lines
of pattern matching against messages YouTube changes; the server already has it, and a copy
here would be a copy going stale. So this sends back the exit code and the output, and the
server decides what to tell the person waiting.

Finished audio is kept in a bounded local cache, so the same video imported twice is fetched
once — see `Cache`, including the two cases where a copy is deliberately not kept.

Nothing outside the standard library is imported, so this file can simply be copied onto a
machine that has python3, yt-dlp and ffmpeg.

Usage:

    export MUSIC_RADIO_SERVER=https://cloud.example.com
    export MUSIC_RADIO_USER=radio-worker
    export MUSIC_RADIO_PASSWORD_FILE=/etc/music-radio/app-password
    music-radio-worker --check       # verify the credentials and the local tools
    music-radio-worker               # collect jobs until stopped

Get the password with `occ user:add-app-password radio-worker` on the server, and put that
account on the allow-list in Settings → Administration → Music Radio.
"""

